TI  - Effects of the interspecific competition between Eichhornia crassipes and Alternanthera philoxeroides and the additional impact of their biological control agents: relevance for aquatic weed management
T2  - HYDROBIOLOGIA
AB  - The introduction of non-native species has led to an increase in co-occurring species in shared habitats, particularly in aquatic ecosystems. This study aimed to elucidate how biological control agents modulate the interspecific competition between the invasive plant species Eichhornia crassipes and Alternanthera philoxeroides while also assessing the impact of their respective biocontrol agents, Neochetina eichhorniae and Agasicles hygrophila. Under controlled conditions, a 60-day experiment was conducted in which the growth, defoliation, relative growth rates, and root-to-shoot ratios of the two species in both monoculture and mixed culture were evaluated in the presence and absence of their biological control agents. The results indicate that E. crassipes consistently outperformed A. philoxeroides in growth under all of the tested treatment conditions, exhibiting greater biomass and higher relative growth rates in monocultures than in mixed cultures (p < 0.05). Defoliation of E. crassipes and A. philoxeroides increased significantly under herbivory (p < 0.05). Pathway analysis revealed that biological control agents had a pronounced negative effect on A. philoxeroides (the standardized path coefficients: - 0.93, p < 0.05), whereas interspecific competition was the most significant factor that suppressed E. crassipes (the standardized path coefficients: - 0.73, p > 0.05). These findings suggest that when biological control agents are present, E. crassipes is likely to gradually dominate and to displace A. philoxeroides within shared habitats. Mechanical removal of E. crassipes may be necessary in the short term, while the development and introduction of additional biocontrol agents could provide a more sustainable solution in the long term. The insights gained from this research provide a scientific basis for developing more effective management strategies to control the invasion of E. crassipes and A. philoxeroides in the riparian and wetland regions of southern China, where the two invasive species and their biological control agents frequently co-occur. Specifically, in addition to mechanical removal, the introduction, development, and enhancement of biological control agents are crucial for the effective restoration of native aquatic ecosystems compromised by multiple invasive species.
