TY  - JOUR
AU  - NORTON, TA
TI  - ECOLOGICAL EXPERIMENTS WITH SARGASSUM-MUTICUM
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE MARINE BIOLOGICAL ASSOCIATION OF THE UNITED KINGDOM
AB  - The habitat and vertical distribution of the Japanese seaweed S. muticum (Yendo) Fensholt was investigated in the San Juan Islands and Washington [USA] where it was introduced over 20 yr ago. In this region it is restricted to the lower shore and the sublittoral fringe. Cultural experiments showed that its upward extension into the littoral zone could be limited by its susceptibility to desiccation and insolation in summer and to frosting in winter. Even sublethal exposures to these factors caused retardation of subsequent growth of the plants, thus diminishing their competitive ability. The degree of retardation increased with the severity and duration of exposure to stress. The penetration of the species into the deeper sublittoral area is controlled by the clarity of the water and by grazing pressure. The local and geographical distribution of the species are influenced by salinity and water temperature. The plants have some tolerance to reduced salinity. In culture, germlings survived at salinities down to 20% and vegetative branches down to 9%. The rate of growth of both was much reduced at the lower tolerated salinities. There is some acclimatization by adult plants to the slightly reduced salinity of the waters around the San Juan Islands. In culture, the growth of both germlings and of vegetative branches was negligible at a water temperature of 0.degree. C, but increased with water temperature over the range tested, (5-25.degree. C). A preference for warmer water may help to explain the species'' restriction to calm bays in the relatively cool waters of the N-W USA and also its accelerating southward spread into warmer waters down the Pacific seaboard of N America. The growth rates of the plants in culture showed that in warmer waters of moderate to high salinity S. muticum is a formidable competitor for any seaweed. This is substantiated by the invasiveness of this species under such conditions in nature.
