TI  - ECOLOGY OF FISHES IN NYUMBA-YA-MUNGU RESERVOIR, TANZANIA
T2  - BIOLOGICAL JOURNAL OF THE LINNEAN SOCIETY
AB  - Twenty species [Mormyrus kannume, Rhabdalestes leleupi, Garra dembeensis, Labeo forskalii, Barbus oxyrhynchus, B. paludinosus, B. kerstenii kerstenii, B. jacksonii, B. lineomaculatus, B. usambarae, Barbus sp., Clarias mossambicus, Synodontis punctulatus, Chiloglanis deckenii, Anguilla nebulosa labiata, Haplochromis bloyeti, Sarotherodon jipe, S. pangani, S. esculentus and Tilapia rendalii] of fish were found in Nyumba ya Mungu reservoir on the River Pangani which, with 2 exceptions, were indigenous to the river basin. Their distribution is described, and some information on their growth, reproduction and feeding habits is presented. The highest fish densities were found in the littoral and the broad, shallow, upstream region of the lake. The lacustrine environment favored the endemic tilapias, S. jipe and S. pangani, which graze periphyton, and the introduced species, S. esculentus which feeds on phytoplankton. Males of the endemic forms may grow to a large size, but the introduced species, including T. rendalli, were heavier for a given length. A reduction in tilapia stocks since 1970 is reflected in experimental catches during 1972-74 and was attributed to the combined effects of heavy commercial exploitation and a loss of spawning grounds. In the same period a striking increase in the populations of small carnivores R. leleupi and H. gr. bloyeti was recorded. Fully plantivorous and piscivorous fish are absent, but omnivores are represented by Barbus spp. and Synodontis punctulatus. Tilapias exhibited a high incidence of nematode infection, and the available evidence indicates that fish-eating birds are important predators of cichlids in Nyumba ya Mungu.
