TI  - FRESH-WATER ZOOPLANKTON COMMUNITY STRUCTURE - INTRODUCTION OF LARGE INVERTEBRATE PREDATORS AND LARGE HERBIVORES TO A SMALL-SPECIES COMMUNITY
T2  - CANADIAN JOURNAL OF FISHERIES AND AQUATIC SCIENCES
AB  - Two hypotheses have been proposed to explain the scarcity of small zooplankton species in fishelss lakes, in which large zooplankton species dominate: (1) selective predation on small species by large invertebrate predators and (2) competitive suppression of small species by large herbivores. These hypotheses were tested at the Experimental Lakes Area (EIA) by introducing a large invertebrate predator, Chaoborus americanus, and a large herbivore, Daphinia catawba, both common in fishless ELA lakes, into a zooplankton community typical of ELA lakes with fish (small zooplankton species present). Chaoborus had much more of an impact than D. catawba on resident zooplankton, although both introduced species significantly reduced the abundance of some resident species. Daphnia galeata mendoate, the dominant species in the absence of introduced species, was reduced to virtual extinction by Chaoborus. The cladocerans Bosmina and Diaphanosoma, the copepods Diaptomus spp., and  the rotifer Conochilus also were reduced in abundance by Chaoborus. Daphnia galeata mendoate and Bosmina were the only resident species consistently reduced in abundance by D. catawba. These results support current conceptual models that depict invertebrate as an important factor responsible for the relative scarcity of small zooplankton species in fishless lakes.
