TI  - UPTAKE AND REGENERATION OF NITROGEN IN CONTROLLED AQUATIC ECOSYSTEMS AND THE EFFECTS OF COPPER ON THESE PROCESSES
T2  - BULLETIN OF MARINE SCIENCE
AB  - Uptake and regeneration of N, and the effects of low concentrations of Cu on these processes, were investigated in large volumes (60 m3) of nearshore marine water enclosed in Controlled Experimental Ecosystems (CEE). 15N-labeled ammonium or nitrate was introduced into CEE and changes in concentrations and 15N content of ammonium, nitrate and particulate N were monitored for 7-10 days. Rates of ammonium regeneration at 1 m ranged from 59-118% of the ammonium uptake by living organisms [including phytoplankton], and they equaled about half of the loss of particulate N. Ammonium uptake for the first 48 h after exposure to about 7 ppb Cu was reduced by about 50%. After this initial depression uptake recovered to near control (no Cu) values. A similar trend was observed for nitrate uptake and 14C productivity. In Cu-treated CEE, the amount of particulate N settling through the water column was substantially less than that in the controls. This was attributed to a decline in zooplankton grazing in the presence of Cu. The effect of Cu on ammonium regeneration was less intensive than it was on ammonium uptake.
