TI  - UTILIZATION OF WILLOW LEAVES, GRASS AND PERIPHYTON BY STREAM MACROINVERTEBRATES - A STUDY USING STABLE CARBON ISOTOPES
T2  - ARCHIV FUR HYDROBIOLOGIE
AB  - Natural carbon isotopes were examined in stream invertebrates and their potential foods, to ascertain the importance of leaves of the introduced willow tree (Salix fragilis) as a food supply in two New Zealand streams. Invertebrates and their potential foods were sampled from two streams, in three seasons, in sites with willow trees and in adjacent sites with tussock grassland vegetation. Autochthonous carbon sources, as indicated by the stone surface organic layer at open sites, were always isotopically distinct from allochthonous grass and willow leaves. At willow shaded-sites, carbon derived from willows was estimated to have contributed up to 66 % of stone surface organic layer carbon, and up to 78 % of the fine particulate organic layer carbon. Late instar insect larvae at willow-shaded sites of Styx Creek were estimated to have derived 8-74 % of their body carbon from allochthonous material, compared with 0-23 % at open sites. Over half of the total body carbon of some genera appeared to be derived from willows. Similar trends were observed in Heeney Creek, with 93 % of the body carbon of the facultative shredder/browser Olinga sp. (Trichoptera: Conoesucidae) being of allochthonous origin. Carbon derived from willows was incorporated by insects in all genera examined in both streams, throughout the year. Even predators at willow-shaded sites derived up to 47 % of their carbon ultimately from willow leaves. Although these and many other New Zealand streams lack any specialised shredders, our results nevertheless indicate that allochthonous material can play a major role in energy flow.
