AU  - Vollhardt, D
AU  - Brezesinski, G
ED  - Wang, C
ED  - Leblanc, RM
TI  - Synchrotron-Based X-ray Methods as Powerful Tools for the Characterization of Monolayers at the Air/Liquid Interface
T2  - RECENT PROGRESS IN COLLOID AND SURFACE CHEMISTRY WITH BIOLOGICAL APPLICATIONS
AB  - Knowledge of the structure and phase behavior of condensed Langmuir monolayers in A-scale progressed rapidly with the development of surface-specific X-ray diffraction techniques and the introduction of high intensity synchrotron X-ray sources. In this chapter, two synchrotron based X-ray methods are discussed: Grazing incidence X-ray diffraction (GIXD), and Total reflection X-ray fluorescence (TRXF). The high potential of synchrotron GIXD is presented on some selected examples to demonstrate the structural characterization for a variety of monolayer model systems of different amphiphiles. Due to the Angstrom resolution, GIXD represents the most important technique for probing the lateral ordering in condensed monolayers at the air/water interface and allows the establishment of phase diagrams of amphiphilic monolayers on the basis of two-dimensional lattice structures and tilt directions of the molecules. The present review highlights selected results of the Angstrom characterization of various single-chain and double-chain amphiphiles (acyl monoglycerols, amide amphiphiles of derivatized ethanolamine, phospholipids, amidolipids), of the effect of unsaturation in fatty acids, of chiral discrimination effects, and of phase transition in adsorbed monolayers. Biomimetic model systems of double-chain phospholipids and the omnipresent triterpenoid oleanolic acid are discussed. The use of TRXF as a surface sensitive analytical tool allows the detection and quantification of specific elements. Using this method, the determination of the protonation degree of new amphiphiles designed for non-viral DNA transfection was possible.
